<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet xml:id="activity-exponentials-log-differentiation">
  <title>Logarithmic Differentiation</title>

  <objectives xml:id="obj-exponentials-log-differentiation">
    <ul>
      <li><p>Use logarithmic differentiation to prove the power rule for every real power and to recover the product rule.</p></li>
      <li><p>Use logarithmic differentiation to differentiate functions such as <m>x^x</m>, where neither the power rule nor the exponential rule applies.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      We know a lot about <m>\ln(x)</m>: it is defined by an integral, it turns changes questions
      about products into questions about sums, and its derivative is <m>1/x</m>.
      In this activity, we will take advantage of the logarithm being an invertible function.
      This will allow us to use <m>\ln</m> to make sense of powers
      <m>b^p</m> when <m>p</m> is not a whole number.
      This will allow us to define exponential functions and then to find their derivatives.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-numerical-logarithms">
    <p>
      Throughout, you may use the approximate values:
      <md>
        <mrow>  \ln(2) \amp \approx 0.693  </mrow>
        <mrow> \ln(3) \amp \approx 1.099 </mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-building-log-scale" workspace="0in">
    <title>Building a Logarithmic Scale</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        On the number line below, each position <m>x</m> is marked underneath. We will write a
        number <m>y</m> <em>above</em> the line at the position <m>x=\ln(y)</m>. Since
        <m>\ln(1)=0</m>, the label <m>y=1</m> has already been placed above <m>x=0</m>.
      </p>
      <image width="95%">
        <shortdescription>
          A horizontal number line running from x = -3.5 to x = 3.5, with major tick marks at
          the integers from -3 to 3 labeled below the line and minor tick marks every 0.1. Above
          the line at x = 0 is the label y = 1. The rest of the space above the line is blank.
        </shortdescription>
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}[x=2.2cm]
            \draw[thick,&lt;-&gt;] (-3.5,0) -- (3.5,0);
            \foreach \i in {-34,...,34} {
              \draw ({\i/10},-0.06) -- ({\i/10},0.06);
            }
            \foreach \i in {-3,...,3} {
              \draw[thick] (\i,-0.15) -- (\i,0.15);
              \node[below] at (\i,-0.15) {$\i$};
            }
            \node[above] at (0,0.15) {$1$};
            \node[left] at (-3.5,0.45) {$y$};
            \node[left] at (-3.5,-0.45) {$x$};
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-scale-powers-of-two" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the power rule for logarithms to find the position <m>x=\ln(y)</m> for
          <m>y=2</m>, <m>4</m>, and <m>8</m>. Label each of these values of <m>y</m> above the
          number line. How far apart are consecutive labels?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-scale-negative-powers" workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now find the positions for <m>y=\frac{1}{2}</m>, <m>\frac{1}{4}</m>, and
          <m>\frac{1}{8}</m>, and label them. On which side of <m>x=0</m> do they fall, and why?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-scale-threes" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Label <m>y=3</m> and <m>y=9</m>, and then <m>y=6</m> and <m>y=12</m> on the number line.
          What happens to values of <m>y</m> when you move to the left or right on <m>x</m>-values
          by the distance <m>\ln(3)</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-scale-find-e" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Is there a number <m>y</m> that belongs above the position <m>x=1</m>? Between which
          two labels must it be? Use the fact that <m>\ln</m> is continuous to explain why such a
          number must exist.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-scale-reach" workspace="0in">
    <title>Does the Scale Reach Every Position?</title>
    <task xml:id="task-scale-reach-ten" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find a power of <m>2</m> whose label sits to the right of <m>x=10</m>, and a power of
          <m>2</m> whose label sits to the left of <m>x=-10</m>. Explain why there must be a
          number <m>y</m> with <m>\ln(y)=10</m> and a number <m>y</m> with <m>\ln(y)=-10</m>.
          Would the same reasoning work for any position <m>p</m> on the number line?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <remark xml:id="rem-ln-range">
    <title>The Range of <m>\ln</m></title>
    <p>
      Because <m>\ln'(x)=\dfrac{1}{x} \gt 0</m>, the function <m>\ln</m> is increasing 
      on <m>(0,\infty)</m>, so no two numbers <m>y</m> are ever placed at the same position.
      Because <m>\ln(2^n)=n\ln(2)</m> can be made as large or as negative as we like,
      and <m>\ln</m> is continuous, every position is filled. In other words,
      the range of <m>\ln</m> is <m>(-\infty,\infty)</m>, and for every
      real number <m>p</m> there is <em>exactly one</em> <m>y\gt 0</m> with <m>\ln(y)=p</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      This also proves two limits:
      <md>
        <mrow> \lim_{x \to 0^+} \ln(x) \amp = -\infty </mrow>
        <mrow> \lim_{x \to \infty} \ln(x) \amp = +\infty </mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <definition xml:id="def-e-and-exponentials">
    <title>The Number <m>e</m> and Real Powers</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The <term>natural base</term> <m>e</m> is that number with <m>\ln(e)=1</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        For every real number <m>p</m>, the power <m>e^p</m> is the number <m>N</m> with
        <md>e^p = N \quad \Leftrightarrow \quad \ln(N)=p</md>.
        More generally, if <m>b \gt 0</m>, the power <m>b^p</m> is the number <m>N</m> with
        <md>b^p = N \quad \Leftrightarrow \quad \ln(N)=p\ln(b)</md>.
        The <term>natural exponential function</term> is <m>\exp(x)=e^x</m>, and the
        <term>exponential function with base <m>b</m></term> is <m>\exp_b(x)=b^x</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-definition-consistency" workspace="0in">
    <title>Does the Definition Agree with What We Know?</title>
    <task xml:id="task-consistency-e-cubed" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using the definition, <m>e^3</m> is the number whose logarithm is <m>3</m>. Use the
          product rule for logarithms to find <m>\ln(e\cdot e\cdot e)</m>. Explain why the
          definition gives the same number <m>e^3</m> as multiplying <m>e</m> by itself three
          times.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-exp-ln-inverse">
    <title><m>e^x</m> and <m>\ln(x)</m> Are Inverse Functions</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For every real number <m>x</m>, <m>\ln(e^x)=x</m>, and for every <m>y\gt 0</m>,
        <m>e^{\ln(y)}=y</m>. Since <m>\ln(y)</m> is differentiable and its derivative
        <m>1/y</m> is never zero, its inverse function <m>e^x</m> is also differentiable. For
        the same reason, <m>b^x</m> is differentiable for every <m>b\gt 0</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-derivative-exponentials" workspace="0in">
    <title>Derivatives of Exponential Functions</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Now that we know <m>e^x</m> and <m>b^x</m> <em>have</em> derivatives,
        we can <em>find</em> them by differentiating the equations that define them
        using implicit differentiation.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-derivative-e-x" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          For <m>y=e^x</m>, differentiate both sides of <m>\ln(y) = x</m> with respect to <m>x</m>.
          Use the chain rule on the left side since <m>y</m> is a function of <m>x</m>. Solve the result for <m>y' = \dfrac{d}{dx}\big[e^x\big]</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-derivative-b-x" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>b\gt 0</m>. For <m>y = b^x</m>, differentiate both sides of 
          <m>\ln(y) = \ln(b^x)=x\ln(b)</m> with respect to <m>x</m>,
          and solve for <m>y' = \dfrac{d}{dx}\big[b^x\big]</m>.
          What happens to this formula when <m>b=e</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-derivative-b-x-sign" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The value <m>b^x</m> is always positive. Use the sign of <m>\ln(b)</m> to decide
          whether <m>b^x</m> is increasing, decreasing, or constant when <m>b\gt 1</m>, when
          <m>0\lt b\lt 1</m>, and when <m>b=1</m>. Do your answers match what you expect for
          <m>2^x</m>, <m>\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^x</m>, and <m>1^x</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <remark xml:id="rem-log-differentiation">
    <title>Logarithmic Differentiation</title>
    <p>
      In the last exercise we found a derivative by taking the logarithm first. This works for
      any differentiable function <m>y=f(x)</m> wherever <m>f(x)\ne 0</m>. Write
      <m>\ln|y|=\ln|f(x)|</m>, use the properties of <m>\ln</m> to expand the right side, and
      differentiate both sides. Since <m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\big[\ln|y|\big]=\dfrac{y'}{y}</m>, we can
      solve for <m>y'</m>:
      <md>y' = y\cdot\frac{d}{dx}\Big[\ln|f(x)|\Big]\text{.}</md>
      This method is called <term>logarithmic differentiation</term>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-log-differentiation-proves" workspace="0in">
    <title>What Logarithmic Differentiation Proves</title>
    <task xml:id="task-logdiff-power-rule" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>p</m> be any real number and let <m>y=x^p</m> for <m>x\gt 0</m>. Use
          logarithmic differentiation to find <m>y'</m>. In Calculus I, the power rule was
          justified only for certain kinds of powers <m>p</m>. Why does this argument work for
          <em>every</em> real number <m>p</m>, such as <m>p=\sqrt{2}</m> or <m>p=\pi</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-logdiff-product-rule" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>y=f(x)\,g(x)</m>, where <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> are differentiable and nonzero.
          Use logarithmic differentiation to find <m>y'</m>, and simplify. Which rule have you
          found?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-using-new-derivatives" workspace="0in">
    <title>Using the New Derivatives</title>
    <task xml:id="task-practice-x2-ex" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the derivative of <m>x^2e^x</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-practice-x-to-x" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>y=x^x</m> for <m>x\gt 0</m>. Explain why neither the power rule nor the rule for
          <m>b^x</m> applies to this function. Then use logarithmic differentiation to find
          <m>y'</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-practice-x-to-lnx" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>y=x^{\ln(x)}</m> for <m>x\gt 0</m>. Use logarithmic differentiation to find
          <m>y'</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
